window.docContents[27].resolve({"/Cliques/#section-finite-cliques":{"contents":"Examples of cliques in a graph\n\nIf s : Finset V is a clique with n vertices then we call it an n-clique. This\nis IsNClique in Lean, which is a structure that bundles a proof that s is a\nclique together with a proof that s has cardinality n.\n\nNotice that IsClique takes a Set V while IsNClique takes a Finset V. A clique\nmay be infinite, so IsClique needs to work with general sets. But an n-clique\nneeds a size in ℕ, and #s is a natural number for a finset (and computable, so\ndecide and #eval can use it). The field isClique : G.IsClique s silently\ncoerces the finset s to a set. This coercion is written ↑s (or (s : Set V)).\n\n#source IsNClique -- structure IsNClique (n : ℕ) (s : Finset α) : Prop where\n                  --   isClique : G.IsClique s\n                  --   card_eq : #s = n\n\n\nThe Finset {a, b} is a 2-clique if and only if G.Adj a b holds.\n\nlemma is2Clique_pair_iff {a b : V} [DecidableEq V] :\n    G.IsNClique 2 {a, b} ↔ G.Adj a b := by\n  constructor <;> intro h\n  · apply h.isClique (by grind) (by grind)\n    rintro rfl\n    grind [h.card_eq]\n  · constructor <;> simp [h, h.ne]\n\n\nThe Finset {a, b, c} is a 3-clique if and only if a, b, and c are\npairwise adjacent. Notice in this and the previous lemma we need to give Lean a\nDecidableEq V instance to be able to form the Finset {a, b} and {a, b, c}.\nAlso we don't need to assume that the vertices in the cliques are distinct because\npairwise adjacency trivially implies that they are distinct.\n\nexample {a b c : V} [DecidableEq V] :\n    G.IsNClique 3 {a, b, c} ↔ G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj b c :=\n  is3Clique_triple_iff\n\n\nIf we insert a vertex a into an n-clique s and a is adjacent to every vertex\nin s then insert a s is an (n + 1)-clique.\n\n#check IsNClique.insert\n\n\nFor concrete finite graphs decide can check small examples directly.\n\nexample : K[9].IsNClique 5 {0, 1, 3, 5, 7} := by decide\n\n\nFamously the Mycielskian of any triangle-free graph is also triangle-free, so in\nparticular any iterated Mycielskian of K[2] is triangle-free. (We will prove this\nand a more general result later.)\n\nFor now we just check that a particular set of 3 vertices in the 3rd iterated\nMycielskian of K[2] (mμ^3(K[2])) is not a 3-clique.\n\nexample :\n    ¬ mμ^3(K[2]).IsNClique 3 {(0 : Fin 2)ʳʳʳ, (1 : Fin 2)ˡˡˡ, (1 : Fin 2)ˡʳˡ} := by\n  decide\n\n\nAny map f : V → W that sends adjacent vertices of a clique s of G to adjacent\nvertices of a graph H is injective on s: distinct vertices of s are adjacent,\nso their images are adjacent, hence distinct (since H is loopless).\n\nlemma IsClique.injOn_of_adj {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set V}\n    (hs : G.IsClique s) {f : V → W}\n    (hadj : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ s → ∀ ⦃y⦄, y ∈ s → G.Adj x y → H.Adj (f x) (f y)) :\n    Set.InjOn f s := by\n  intro x hx y hy hxy\n  by_contra hne\n  exact (hadj hx hy (hs hx hy hne)).ne hxy\n\n\nIn particular a graph homomorphism G →g H is injective on any clique. This\ngeneralizes Hom.injective_of_top_hom (the case where the graph is a clique).\n\nlemma Hom.injOn_of_isClique {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (f : G →g H)\n    {s : Set V} (hs : G.IsClique s) : Set.InjOn f s :=\n  hs.injOn_of_adj fun _ _ _ _ h => f.map_adj h\n\n\nHence the image of an n-clique s under such a map is an n-clique of H. We\nwill use this several times, with f a graph homomorphism or a projection.\n\nIf a map f : V → W sends adjacent vertices of an n-clique s of G to\nadjacent vertices of H then the image of s is an n-clique of H./-- If a map f : V → W sends adjacent vertices of an n-clique s of G to\nadjacent vertices of H then the image of s is an n-clique of H. -/\nlemma IsNClique.image_of_adj {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} [DecidableEq W]\n    {n : ℕ} {s : Finset V} (hs : G.IsNClique n s) {f : V → W}\n    (hadj : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ s → ∀ ⦃y⦄, y ∈ s → G.Adj x y → H.Adj (f x) (f y)) :\n    H.IsNClique n (s.image f) := by\n  have hinj := hs.isClique.injOn_of_adj hadj\n  refine ⟨?_, by rw [Finset.card_image_of_injOn hinj, hs.card_eq]⟩\n  rintro _ ha _ hb hne\n  obtain ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩ := Finset.mem_image.mp ha\n  obtain ⟨y, hy, rfl⟩ := Finset.mem_image.mp hb\n  exact hadj hx hy (hs.isClique hx hy fun h => hne (by rw [h]))\n\n\nThe image of an n-clique under a graph homomorphism is an n-clique./-- The image of an n-clique under a graph homomorphism is an n-clique. -/\nprotected lemma IsNClique.image {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} [DecidableEq W]\n    {n : ℕ} {s : Finset V} (hs : G.IsNClique n s) (f : G →g H) :\n    H.IsNClique n (s.image f) :=\n  hs.image_of_adj fun _ _ _ _ h => f.map_adj h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques","header":"6.2. Finite Cliques","id":"/Cliques/#section-finite-cliques"},"/Colorings/#section-exercises-colorings":{"contents":"1. Show that the cycle graph C[5] is not 2-colourable.\n\nend SimpleGraph\nend\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nopen Finset\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\nexample : ¬ C[5].Colorable 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that if G has an edge then 2 ≤ χ G. Hint: the endpoints of an edge form\na clique, so you can use IsClique.card_le_chromaticNumber.\n\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : 2 ≤ χ G := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Show that a graph G on n vertices satisfies n ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ. Hint: combine\nnatCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum with cliqueNum_compl.\n\nexample : Nat.card V ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Show that the chromatic number of the hypercube Q[n + 1] is 2. Hint:\nchromaticNumber_eq_two_iff reduces this to facts proved in this chapter.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : χ Q[n + 1] = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Recall the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]: the graph whose vertices are the k-element\nsubsets of Fin n = {0, ..., n - 1} with edges between disjoint subsets. Show that\nit has an (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring for 2 * k ≤ n and 0 < k. The colouring is\ngiven below: colour a vertex by its least element, merging all the large colours into\none. This is the hardest exercise in this chapter: the case where both vertices\nreceive the merged colour needs a pigeonhole argument which is in Mathlib as\nFinset.inter_nonempty_of_card_lt_card_add_card.\n\nThe colouring below for the Petersen graph \\mathrm{Kn}(5,2), with 5 - 2 \\cdot 2 + 2 = 3 colours: a vertex is red if its least element is 0, blue if its least element is 1, and green (the merged colour) otherwise\n\nThe (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]./-- The (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]. -/\ndef kneserGraph.coloring' (n k : ℕ) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n) (hk : 0 < k) :\n    Kn[n, k].Coloring (Fin (n - 2 * k + 2)) where\n  toFun s :=\n    -- A vertex is s : Finset (Fin n) of size k. Since 0 < k, s is\n    -- nonempty. We colour s by its minimum element m.\n    let m : Fin n := s.val.min' (card_pos.mp (by rw [s.property]; exact hk))\n    -- Minima below n - 2 * k + 1 keep their own colour; every larger minimum\n    -- shares the last colour n - 2 * k + 1.\n    if h : m.val < n - 2 * k + 1 then\n      ⟨m.val, by grind⟩\n    else\n      ⟨n - 2 * k + 1, by grind⟩\n  map_rel' := by\n    sorry\n\n\n6. Show that if 0 < k, 2 * k ≤ n and Kn[n, k] is m-colourable then\nn ≤ k * m, so that χ Kn[n, k] ≥ n / k. Hint: use\nColorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum and the independence number of the Kneser graph,\nindepNum_kneserGraph, from the previous chapter; Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq will\nhelp with the binomial coefficients.\n\ntheorem le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph' {n k m : ℕ} (hk : 0 < k) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n)\n    (h : Kn[n, k].Colorable m) : n ≤ k * m := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Use the previous two exercises to show that the Petersen graph Kn[5, 2] has\nchromatic number 3.\n\nexample : χ Kn[5, 2] = 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Prove the claim from the chapter that for G : SimpleGraph ℕ a vertex v has at\nmost v earlier neighbours.\n\nlemma degreeLT_le_self' (G : SimpleGraph ℕ) (v : ℕ) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] :\n    G.degreeLT v ≤ v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. The infinite star starNat joins 0 to every other natural number. Show that\n0 has infinitely many neighbours, so that starNat is not locally finite and the\nbound colorable_of_degree_le_of_countable does not apply, but that starNat is\nnevertheless 2-colourable, using colorable_of_degreeLT_le: every vertex has at\nmost one earlier neighbour.\n\nThe infinite star on ℕ: the vertex 0 is adjacent to every other vertex./-- The infinite star on ℕ: the vertex 0 is adjacent to every other vertex. -/\nabbrev starNat : SimpleGraph ℕ where\n  Adj a b := (a = 0 ∧ b ≠ 0) ∨ (b = 0 ∧ a ≠ 0)\n\n\nexample : (starNat.neighborSet 0).Infinite ∧ starNat.Colorable 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that the greedy colouring of the complete graph on ℕ colours vertex n\nwith colour n. (See greedy_crownGraph_eq for a similar proof using\nNat.strong_induction_on.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy n = n := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that the cycle graph C[5] is not 2-colourable.example : ¬ C[5].Colorable 2 := by\n  rw [← chromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable,\n    chromaticNumber_cycleGraph_of_odd 5 (by decide) (by decide)]\n  decide\n2. Show that if G has an edge then 2 ≤ χ G. Hint: the endpoints of an edge form\na clique, so you can use IsClique.card_le_chromaticNumber.example {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : 2 ≤ χ G := by\n  classical\n  have hc : G.IsClique ({u, v} : Finset V) := by simpa using fun _ => h\n  simpa [card_pair h.ne] using hc.card_le_chromaticNumber\n3. Show that a graph G on n vertices satisfies n ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ. Hint: combine\nnatCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum with cliqueNum_compl.example : Nat.card V ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ :=\n  -- an independent set of G is a clique of Gᶜ\n  calc (Nat.card V : ℕ∞) ≤ χ G * ⍺ G := natCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum\n    _ = χ G * ω Gᶜ := by rw [cliqueNum_compl]\n    -- gcongr (\"generalized congruence\") proves an inequality between two terms of\n    -- the same shape by comparing the parts that differ: since multiplication by\n    -- χ G is monotone, it reduces the goal to ω Gᶜ ≤ χ Gᶜ\n    _ ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ := by gcongr; exact cliqueNum_le_chromaticNumber\n4. Show that the chromatic number of the hypercube Q[n + 1] is 2. Hint:\nchromaticNumber_eq_two_iff reduces this to facts proved in this chapter.example (n : ℕ) : χ Q[n + 1] = 2 := by\n  -- bipartiteness was proved in this chapter, so it only remains to show\n  -- Q[n + 1] ≠ ⊥ for which we need a vertex of Q[n] (each gives an edge in\n  -- the next dimension)\n  obtain ⟨v⟩ : Nonempty (HypercubeVert n) := inferInstance\n  refine chromaticNumber_eq_two_iff.2 ⟨IsBipartite.hypercube _, ?_⟩\n  exact ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.2 ⟨(v, false), (v, true), by simp⟩\n5. Recall the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]: the graph whose vertices are the k-element\nsubsets of Fin n = {0, ..., n - 1} with edges between disjoint subsets. Show that\nit has an (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring for 2 * k ≤ n and 0 < k. The colouring is\ngiven below: colour a vertex by its least element, merging all the large colours into\none. This is the hardest exercise in this chapter: the case where both vertices\nreceive the merged colour needs a pigeonhole argument which is in Mathlib as\nFinset.inter_nonempty_of_card_lt_card_add_card.The (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]./-- The (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]. -/\ndef kneserGraph.coloring (n k : ℕ) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n) (hk : 0 < k) :\n    Kn[n, k].Coloring (Fin (n - 2 * k + 2)) where\n  toFun s :=\n    -- A vertex is s : Finset (Fin n) of size k. Since 0 < k, s is\n    -- nonempty. We colour s by its minimum element m.\n    let m : Fin n := s.val.min' (card_pos.mp (by rw [s.property]; exact hk))\n    -- Minima below n - 2 * k + 1 keep their own colour; every larger minimum\n    -- shares the last colour n - 2 * k + 1.\n    if h : m.val < n - 2 * k + 1 then\n      ⟨m.val, by grind⟩\n    else\n      ⟨n - 2 * k + 1, by grind⟩\n  map_rel' := by\n    intro u v hadj hcolor\n    simp only [kneserGraph_adj, ne_eq, Order.lt_add_one_iff] at hadj hcolor\n    split_ifs at hcolor with h1 h2 h3\n    · -- the colour is the common minimum of u and v, which are disjoint\n      have hune : u.val.Nonempty := card_pos.mp (by rw [u.2]; exact hk)\n      have hvne : v.val.Nonempty := card_pos.mp (by rw [v.2]; exact hk)\n      have h : u.val.min' hune = v.val.min' hvne :=\n        Fin.val_injective (by simpa using congrArg Fin.val hcolor)\n      exact Finset.disjoint_left.mp hadj.1 (min'_mem _ hune) (h ▸ min'_mem _ hvne)\n    · grind -- impossible: one colour is ≤ n - 2 * k, the other is n - 2 * k + 1\n    · grind -- impossible: one colour is ≤ n - 2 * k, the other is n - 2 * k + 1\n    · -- push Not pushes the negations inwards (¬ a ≤ b becomes b < a)\n      push Not at h1 h3\n      -- both u and v lie in {n - 2 * k + 1, ..., n - 1}, which has only\n      -- 2 * k - 1 elements, so they lie in an independent set of Kn[n, k]\n      -- (isIndepSet_kneserGraph_of_card_lt from the previous chapter)\n      let a : Fin n := ⟨n - 2 * k, by grind⟩\n      have hsub (s : Finset (Fin n)) (hne : s.Nonempty)\n          (hs : n - 2 * k < ↑(s.min' hne)) : s ⊆ Ioi a :=\n        fun x hx => mem_Ioi.2 (hs.trans_le (min'_le _ x hx))\n      have hind := isIndepSet_kneserGraph_of_card_lt (k := k) (s := Ioi a)\n        (by grind [Fin.card_Ioi])\n      exact hind (hsub _ _ h1) (hsub _ _ h3) hadj.2 hadj\n6. Show that if 0 < k, 2 * k ≤ n and Kn[n, k] is m-colourable then\nn ≤ k * m, so that χ Kn[n, k] ≥ n / k. Hint: use\nColorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum and the independence number of the Kneser graph,\nindepNum_kneserGraph, from the previous chapter; Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq will\nhelp with the binomial coefficients.theorem le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph {n k m : ℕ} (hk : 0 < k) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n)\n    (h : Kn[n, k].Colorable m) : n ≤ k * m := by\n  have : NeZero k := ⟨hk.ne'⟩\n  -- Kn[n, k] has n.choose k vertices and independence number\n  -- (n - 1).choose (k - 1)\n  have hcard := h.natCard_le_mul_indepNum\n  have hkn : k ≤ n / 2 := by grind\n  simp only [indepNum_kneserGraph, hkn, ↓reduceIte, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card,\n    Fintype.card_finset_len, Fintype.card_fin] at hcard\n  -- n * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) = n.choose k * k\n  have key := Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq (n - 1) (k - 1)\n  rw [Nat.sub_add_cancel (by grind), Nat.sub_add_cancel hk] at key\n  have hpos : 0 < (n - 1).choose (k - 1) := Nat.choose_pos (by grind)\n  refine Nat.le_of_mul_le_mul_right ?_ hpos\n  calc n * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) = k * n.choose k := by rw [key, mul_comm]\n    _ ≤ k * (m * (n - 1).choose (k - 1)) := Nat.mul_le_mul_left k hcard\n    _ = k * m * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) := by ring\n7. Use the previous two exercises to show that the Petersen graph Kn[5, 2] has\nchromatic number 3.example : χ Kn[5, 2] = 3 :=\n  -- kneserGraph.coloring uses 5 - 2 * 2 + 2 = 3 colours, and 2 colours would\n  -- need 5 ≤ 2 * 2\n  chromaticNumber_eq_iff_colorable_not_colorable.2\n    ⟨by simpa using (kneserGraph.coloring 5 2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)).colorable,\n     fun h => by\n       simpa using le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph (by norm_num) (by norm_num) h⟩\n8. Prove the claim from the chapter that for G : SimpleGraph ℕ a vertex v has at\nmost v earlier neighbours.lemma degreeLT_le_self (G : SimpleGraph ℕ) (v : ℕ) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] :\n    G.degreeLT v ≤ v := by\n  -- We want to change the 2nd occurrence of v to #(range v) in the goal,\n  -- but rw will change all occurrences, so we use nth_rw 2 below\n  nth_rw 2 [← Finset.card_range v]\n  apply Finset.card_le_card\n  intro x hx; simp_all\n9. The infinite star starNat joins 0 to every other natural number. Show that\n0 has infinitely many neighbours, so that starNat is not locally finite and the\nbound colorable_of_degree_le_of_countable does not apply, but that starNat is\nnevertheless 2-colourable, using colorable_of_degreeLT_le: every vertex has at\nmost one earlier neighbour.example : (starNat.neighborSet 0).Infinite ∧ starNat.Colorable 2 := by\n  refine ⟨Set.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem (f := Nat.succ) Nat.succ_injective\n    (by simp), starNat.colorable_of_degreeLT_le (Δ := 1) fun v => ?_⟩\n  -- the only earlier neighbour that a vertex can have is 0\n  rw [degreeLT, Finset.card_le_one]\n  intro a ha b hb\n  rw [mem_neighborLTFinset] at ha hb\n  grind\n10. Show that the greedy colouring of the complete graph on ℕ colours vertex n\nwith colour n. (See greedy_crownGraph_eq for a similar proof using\nNat.strong_induction_on.)example (n : ℕ) : (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy n = n := by\n  -- as in greedy_crownGraph_eq we use strong induction on n\n  induction n using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n  | _ n ih =>\n  -- the earlier neighbours of n are {0, ..., n - 1}, and by induction they use\n  -- exactly the colours {0, ..., n - 1}\n  refine (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy_eq_of_image_eq_range ?_\n  ext c\n  grind [mem_neighborLTFinset, top_adj]\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.9. Exercises","id":"/Colorings/#section-exercises-colorings"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-order":{"contents":"There is a partial order on SimpleGraph V given by G ≤ H iff G is a subgraph of\nH. Note that these are necessarily spanning subgraphs since all\nSimpleGraph V have the same vertex set. We will consider general subgraphs later,\nsee Subgraphs.\n\nThis gives SimpleGraph V the structure of a complete atomic Boolean algebra, with\noperations sup (⊔), inf (⊓), compl (ᶜ) and sdiff (\\), and elements\ntop (⊤) and bot (⊥). This terminology comes from order theory rather than\ngraph theory, but it is very natural in the context of formalization. Here complete\nmeans that every set of graphs in SimpleGraph V has a supremum (the graph whose\nedges are those present in at least one graph in the set) and an infimum (the graph\nwhose edges are those present in every graph in the set). The atoms of\nSimpleGraph V are the graphs with exactly one edge, and atomic means that every\ngraph is the supremum of the atoms below it, i.e. the union of its single-edge\nsubgraphs. Mathlib registers this structure as an instance, which Lean supplies\nautomatically wherever it is needed. The command #synth asks Lean to find\n(synthesize) an instance and shows it; we will say more about instances in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.\n\nsection order\n\n#synth CompleteAtomicBooleanAlgebra (SimpleGraph V)\n\n\n#source SimpleGraph.instLE -- le x y := ∀ ⦃v w : V⦄, x.Adj v w → y.Adj v w\n\n\nvariable (H : SimpleGraph V)\n#check G ≤ H\n\n\nThe definition of proper subgraph (G < H) is phrased differently to how we might\nexpect. Rather than G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ G ≠ H we have:\n\nexample : G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ ¬ H ≤ G := by rfl\n\n\nOf course they are equivalent.\n\nexample : G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ G ≠ H := lt_iff_le_and_ne\n\n\nThe sup of G and H, denoted G ⊔ H, is the SimpleGraph V in which any pair\nof vertices from V are adjacent iff they are adjacent in G or H (i.e. it is the\nunion of G and H).\n\n#check sup_adj\n\n\nThe inf of G and H, denoted G ⊓ H, is the SimpleGraph V in which any pair\nof vertices from V are adjacent iff they are adjacent in both G and H (i.e. it\nis the intersection of G and H).\n\nTop: graphs G (blue) and H (red) on the same vertices.Bottom: their inf G \\sqcap H (left) and sup G \\sqcup H (right), with the edges in both graphs in green\n\n#check inf_adj\n\n\nThe complement of G, denoted Gᶜ, is the SimpleGraph V in which the edges are\nall pairs of distinct vertices that are not adjacent in G.\n\n#check compl_adj\n\n\nThe set difference of two graphs, denoted G \\ H, has the edges of G with the\nedges of H removed. (Since the resulting graph is a SimpleGraph V the vertices\nare necessarily unchanged.)\n\n#check sdiff_adj\n\n\nTop: graphs G and H. Bottom: the complement G^c (left) and the set difference G \\setminus H (right)\n\nWe can remove a set of edges s : Set (Sym2 V) from a graph G to form\nG.deleteEdges s. If s = H.edgeSet then this is the same as the set difference\nG \\ H.\n\n#source deleteEdges\n#check deleteEdges_adj      -- (G.deleteEdges s).Adj v w ↔ G.Adj v w ∧ s(v, w) ∉ s\n#check deleteEdges_edgeSet  -- G.deleteEdges H.edgeSet = G \\ H\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.6. Order","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-order"},"/Walks/#section-circuits-and-cycles":{"contents":"A circuit is a closed trail p (i.e. a walk from a vertex to itself without\nrepeated edges) that is not nil.\n\n#source Walk.IsCircuit -- Prop extends isTrail : IsTrail p where\n                       --               ne_nil : p ≠ nil\n\n\nA cycle is defined in Mathlib as a circuit in which the tail of its support has\nno repeated vertices.\n\n#source Walk.IsCycle  -- Prop extends isCircuit : IsCircuit p where\n                      --          support_nodup : p.support.tail.Nodup\n\n\nA circuit that is not a cycle, and a cycle, in the same graph\n\nAny cycle is a circuit.\n\nexample (c : G.Walk u u) (hc : c.IsCycle) : c.IsCircuit := hc.isCircuit\n\n\nSince a cycle is a circuit, it is not equal to nil.\n\nexample (c : G.Walk u u) (hc : c.IsCycle) : c ≠ nil := hc.isCircuit.ne_nil\n\n\nThere is a 3-cycle in the complete graph K[4].\n\nexample : ∃ (c₃ : K[4].Walk 0 0), c₃.IsCycle ∧ c₃.length = 3 := by\n  let c₃: K[4].Walk 0 0 := cons' 0 1 _ (by decide) <|\n            cons (by decide) <| cons' 2 0 _ (by decide) nil\n  exact ⟨c₃, by simp [c₃, isCycle_def, isTrail_def]⟩\n\n\nThe cycle c₃ defined above and pictured below is cons applied three times: it\nfollows the edges 0 — 1, 1 — 2 and 2 — 0, so it is the cycle 0 1 2 0. The\nremaining vertex 3 of K[4] is not used.\n\nThe 3-cycle 0 1 2 0 in the complete graph K_4\n\nA closed walk is a cycle if and only if it has length at least 3 and its tail is a\npath.\n\n#check isCycle_iff_isPath_tail_and_le_length\n\n\nIf c is a cycle then getVert is injective on {i | 1 ≤ i ∧ i ≤ c.length}.\n\n#check IsCycle.getVert_injOn\n\n\nSo when is a cons h p walk a cycle? Clearly we need p to be a path, and also h\nmust be the adjacency from the end of p to the start of p (otherwise the new walk\nis not closed). Given these two hypotheses, how could cons h p fail to be a cycle?\nWell, we certainly have (cons h p).support.tail.Nodup since this is just\np.support.Nodup and p is a path. Also a cons walk is never nil. So the\nfailure must be that while p is a trail, cons h p is not a trail. This can only\nhappen if the edge corresponding to h is already present. Mathlib has this as\ncons_isCycle_iff.\n\n#check cons_isCycle_iff --  (cons h p).IsCycle ↔ p.IsPath ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges\n\n\nIn fact the only way that the path p could already contain this edge is if p is\nthe walk consisting of the single edge h.symm. This gives the following variant of\ncons_isCycle_iff, which we will need later: cons h p is a cycle iff p is a path\nof length not equal to 1. (The simp only call in the proof uses tail_cons and\nisPath_copy, which involve Walk.copy: see Copy.)\n\nlemma Walk.cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one {u v : V} (p : G.Walk v u)\n    (h : G.Adj u v) : (cons h p).IsCycle ↔ p.IsPath ∧ p.length ≠ 1 := by\n  have h0 : p.length ≠ 0 := fun h0 => h.ne (p.eq_of_length_eq_zero h0).symm\n  simp only [isCycle_iff_isPath_tail_and_le_length, getVert_cons_succ, tail_cons,\n    isPath_copy, length_cons, Nat.reduceLeDiff, ne_eq, and_congr_right_iff]\n  grind\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Trails, Paths, Circuits and Cycles","header":"8.4.3. Circuits and Cycles","id":"/Walks/#section-circuits-and-cycles"}});